Yes—if by “master both” we mean master the narrative layer and the reality layer together.

Narrative gives you meaning, coordination, identity, incentives, framing, and momentum. Reality gives you constraints, causality, consequences, and the parts that do not care what story anyone prefers. The skill is in keeping them coupled without confusing one for the other.

A good operating rule is:

Frame boldly. Test ruthlessly. Update quickly.

If a narrative changes behavior, track the behavior. If a theory predicts an outcome, check the outcome. If the world pushes back, revise the map rather than protecting the story.

That is how you stay grounded while still using narrative as a tool instead of becoming trapped inside it.